## The steps

Designed by this tester (there is no authored step text -- see above). Each step says what was
sent, what came back verbatim, and its verdict.

### STEP 1 -- the DUT has a telnet CLIENT, and what its surface is

**Tool:** `tools/tb qmark tb277 ckA1159 u2 115200 "telnet" "telnet 10.38.22.98" "telnet ip"
"telnet ip 10.38.22.98"`. `qmark.py` types `<prefix> ?`, prints the completions and clears the
line with Ctrl-U **without ever sending a CR**, so nothing is executed (memory
`never-send-cli-help-through-a-cr-driver`). Its own self-test ran first and passed:
`self-test OK: 'show clock ?' then Ctrl-U then CR executed nothing`.

```
awplus# telnet ?
  WORD  IPv4/IPv6 address or hostname of a remote system
  ip    IP telnet
  ipv6  IPv6 telnet
  vrf   VRF instance

awplus# telnet 10.38.22.98 ?
  PORT  TCP Port number
  <cr>

awplus# telnet ip ?
  WORD  IPv4/IPv6 address or hostname of a remote system

awplus# telnet ip 10.38.22.98 ?
  PORT  TCP Port number
  <cr>
```

So the client exists at privileged exec, it has an explicit **IPv4** form (`telnet ip <WORD>`),
an IPv6 form, a VRF form and an optional **TCP port**. This is the surface TPS row 228
"Telnet Client(IPv4)" names.

⚠️ **Documentation nit worth one line:** under `telnet ip`, the help for the address argument
still reads *"IPv4/IPv6 address or hostname of a remote system"* although the `ip` keyword has
just restricted it to IPv4. Cosmetic; the command itself behaves correctly (STEP 4 and STEP 6).

**STEP 1 verdict: PASS** -- the client surface exists and includes an explicit IPv4 form.

### STEP 2 -- on the wire: the client really originates IPv4 TCP to port 23

This step is graded on frame-level evidence, not on a `show` (STANDING-ORDERS section 7, and
`platforms/x960.md` section 7F.2, where `show telnet` read Enabled while IPv4 TCP/23 was
refused). The observation point is the testbox NIC **eth3** (10.38.22.33), which is in the
DUT's `vlan10` and so sees anything the DUT sends to that subnet.

**The control first, same instrument, nothing sent** -- `tools/tb capture tb277 ckA1159 eth3 10
t33379-ctrl.pcap tcp port 23`:
```
0 packets captured / 0 packets received by filter / 0 packets dropped by kernel
file /tmp/ckA1159/t33379-ctrl.pcap: 0 packet(s) total, 0 TCP flow(s) on port 23
```
**Then the leg** -- a 35 s capture on eth3 with the same filter, during which the DUT was told
to telnet to the testbox, which runs no telnet server:

```
tcpdump: listening on eth3, link-type EN10MB (Ethernet), capture size 262144 bytes
2 packets captured
2 packets received by filter
0 packets dropped by kernel
```

`tools/tb tool tb277 ckA1159 tcpflow /tmp/ckA1159/t33379-refused.pcap --port 23`:
```
file /tmp/ckA1159/t33379-refused.pcap: 2 packet(s) total, 1 TCP flow(s) on port 23

flow 10.38.22.33:23 <-> 10.38.22.34:33506  packets=2  duration=0.000s  => REFUSED (SYN answered by RST)
   10.38.22.34:33506 -> 10.38.22.33:23  pkts=1  payload=0 B  flags={SYN}
   10.38.22.33:23 -> 10.38.22.34:33506  pkts=1  payload=0 B  flags={RST.ACK}
```

The control gave 0 and the leg gave 2 on the same instrument, so the zero is a measurement and
not a dead capture (memory `tcpdump-nfs-root-squash-capture`). The capture process had exited
before the file was read -- `tools/tb capture` wraps tcpdump in `timeout`, and the background
job reported its expiry (exit 124) first.

**What this proves:** the DUT's telnet client emits a real **IPv4 TCP SYN to port 23**, sourced
from `10.38.22.34`, the address of the egress SVI `vlan10`; and the testbox answered `RST.ACK`,
which is a refusal, not silence. A client that never emitted anything, and a client whose
packet was lost, both look different from this.

**STEP 2 verdict: PASS.**

### STEP 3 -- end to end: a real telnet login to a real telnet server, and what the far end saw

**Tool:** `tools/tb devclient tb277 ckA1159 u2 115200 --proto telnet --target 10.38.22.98
--user cktmp --password-stdin --cmd "show system" --cmd "show users" --cmd "show telnet"
--expect user --login-timeout 25`, with the throwaway password piped in on **stdin** (it is
therefore not in `ps`; confirmed by reading the DUT-side `ps` line during the next leg, which
shows `--password-stdin` and no value).

**At the same time**, and on a different console, the far end captured the session with its own
`tcpdump` (`platforms/x960.md` section 7B.2): `tools/tb ckcon tb277 ckA1159 u3 9600
"tcpdump -i vlan60 -nn -c 20 tcp port 23"`.

**(a) The DUT side, verbatim:**

