### New tool added by this case: `tools/devclient.py`

A device's **outbound** telnet/SSH client had no driver in `tools/`. `netlogin.py` drives the
testbox's client INTO a device, which is the wrong direction for a "Telnet Client" or
"SSH Client" case, and `conlogin.py` drives a serial console. `devclient.py` is the outbound
twin: it logs in on the console, sends `telnet [ip|ipv6] <host> [PORT]` or
`ssh [ip|ipv6] [port N] [user U] <host>`, answers the far end's login and password prompts,
runs commands ON THE FAR END, can hold the session open, logs out, and **always recovers the
console** -- with the telnet `Ctrl-]`/`quit` and the ssh `~.` escapes as the fallback. It
grades the outcome `PRIV|USER|FAIL|REFUSED|TIMEOUT`, with an exit code per outcome and an
`--expect` check. The password can be passed on **stdin** (`--password-stdin`) so it never
appears in `ps`. Catalogued in `tools/README.md`, and registered with `tools/tb` so it runs as
`tools/tb devclient <TB> <scratch> <uN> <baud> ...`.

Also changed, in the same commit as the group setup: `tools/tb` now forwards
`CK_USER` / `CK_PASSWORD` / `CK_USER_U<N>` / `CK_PASSWORD_U<N>` to the box **over stdin**,
because ssh forwards no environment and a password on the remote command line is readable by
every other user on this shared box. That is what let swi_b (u3), which is on a non-default
password, be driven at all.

